WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:11.060
Cloud secret stores, such as Azure Key Vault at a high level, are designed to centralize storage, access control, monitoring, and rotation support for sensitive values.

2
00:00:11.160 --> 00:00:18.360
Instead of embedding a token in every script, automation can request the secret from a managed service under a controlled identity.

3
00:00:18.460 --> 00:00:28.620
That centralization makes it easier to restrict access, review usage, rotate values, and remove a secret from service without searching through every script directory.

4
00:00:28.900 --> 00:00:34.020
Certificate-based authentication can be stronger than password-based automation when implemented carefully.

5
00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:44.040
Certificates still require lifecycle management: private key protection, expiration tracking, renewal procedures, ownership, and access control.

6
00:00:44.140 --> 00:00:50.700
A certificate stored loosely on an administrator workstation can become just another unmanaged secret.

7
00:00:50.800 --> 00:00:57.440
The advantage comes when certificate issuance, storage, and use are controlled through a well-defined process.

8
00:00:57.720 --> 00:01:06.040
App registrations and service principals can support automation against cloud APIs, but broad permissions and long-lived client secrets create risk.

9
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:12.860
Role-based access control and access policies should be scoped to the work the automation actually performs.

10
00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:19.680
Short-lived credentials, federated identity, and managed identities can reduce reliance on shared secrets.

11
00:01:19.780 --> 00:01:25.220
The best credential is often the one the script never needs to store or handle directly.

12
00:01:25.500 --> 00:01:33.020
Managed identities are especially useful for cloud-hosted automation because the platform can provide an identity to the workload.

13
00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:40.640
The script uses that identity to access approved resources, such as a vault, without embedding a reusable credential.

14
00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.780
This does not remove the need for access review.

15
00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:48.920
The identity still needs least privilege, monitoring, and ownership.

16
00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:56.300
It does, however, reduce the number of long-lived secrets that can be copied into scripts and artifacts.

17
00:01:56.400 --> 00:02:05.920
Centralized certificate and secret lifecycles make access restriction, usage review, rotation, and removal verifiable.
